#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e22ff208-ff10-5106-8922-26adc9007e40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The First Column Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd47d971-5837-5f99-8f4b-a30807e74c19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383078} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b7051e-4f4c-5858-81c6-7e35faa3eedd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383079} {:answer/id #uuid "84db8a33-7747-5b76-be0d-4b2aa383a6ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383080} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9f5f81-1565-5c82-b731-9cc66b0f755f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383081} {:answer/id #uuid "71d017e0-92ef-577a-b8d3-fb05e699815b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84db8a33-7747-5b76-be0d-4b2aa383a6ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383080} :answer-field/id #uuid "0db52104-ea70-537f-b25c-8bdd42a3df6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6fa298d-e031-597f-979d-aed13827cba2" :question/math-academy-id "q-109363" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-(-3)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383083 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e22ff208-ff10-5106-8922-26adc9007e40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The First Column Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d6e8d0e-bc72-52ed-8997-5def469d56aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383085} {:answer/id #uuid "e411059c-22d3-5458-b1cb-d33e346481c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383086} {:answer/id #uuid "6d8a42f5-01b8-54c5-94e5-db391922a389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383087} {:answer/id #uuid "0a3d3a84-3437-5fd4-a8c4-3ab87ac717a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383088} {:answer/id #uuid "dc9a2e83-dbb4-54de-8993-3d9de45971b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e411059c-22d3-5458-b1cb-d33e346481c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383086} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba411dab-2bcf-56d7-a735-d9c328747d71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a9ad7e9-2167-5f2b-a23a-1410cf8a1a4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-109371" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac67bb72-7802-547d-b9d9-ab238cc3768f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The Second Column Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "485e6cc2-3c99-5de5-b384-84205b78d17e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383092} {:answer/id #uuid "fd988ef5-dd5c-52a2-95c2-24c8b53237de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383093} {:answer/id #uuid "8717b111-84dc-5df4-aea7-6e447de2bd46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383094} {:answer/id #uuid "48644ac9-8a1f-5807-94e5-b8150d221ba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383095} {:answer/id #uuid "16fff0d5-669a-5b54-aef9-5ba2062a4b52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd988ef5-dd5c-52a2-95c2-24c8b53237de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383093} :answer-field/id #uuid "da3d2e46-6ec3-525d-a76e-989a5263809e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81db454e-fc31-5d6d-9802-daa3e98c0dc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-109380" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a-b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-2I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-(2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-2I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=1+{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=2-1=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac67bb72-7802-547d-b9d9-ab238cc3768f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The Second Column Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "486780a7-85d7-5dfe-b5d1-b2026694cd8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383099} {:answer/id #uuid "421599be-59ed-5dd2-9764-610e45c0ba23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383100} {:answer/id #uuid "a6520f12-e5cf-5b24-a374-b0318dcbdae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383101} {:answer/id #uuid "75772603-4ddb-5dd8-8089-5e9ad6d88c15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383102} {:answer/id #uuid "8a9806f6-0404-5637-b10a-0aad11e86b17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75772603-4ddb-5dd8-8089-5e9ad6d88c15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383102} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ef8bd2f-e394-56f3-a8d5-d0d1b37d682f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0ed3cda8-bd60-5b95-aaae-3c1474b47878" :question/math-academy-id "q-109383" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a+2b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=7.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-7I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-7I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]-(7)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-7I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-1-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+2b=(-3)+2\\cdot 1=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53e40a15-120c-5587-979b-cc1369d244bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Columns of a Transformation Matrix in a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db7bcd8-832e-5d97-8749-a9e289a75e52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383106} {:answer/id #uuid "6713c2ea-1d17-5222-acd4-dd1873f9144c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383107} {:answer/id #uuid "9bc195bd-82c1-59d6-946b-67d54caf0708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383108} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c9af8b-2b4b-53be-91f8-32c2f07040af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383109} {:answer/id #uuid "5a47036e-cb53-5462-b242-b2e03ebd0a07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ and $x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0db7bcd8-832e-5d97-8749-a9e289a75e52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383106} :answer-field/id #uuid "68622d02-0ae4-553d-9039-bde54e5bcea1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0c6609c-9527-58c5-bfce-a9477772e0fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-112483" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(2).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(2) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $z$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-2I)z=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."}]}
